1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,600
You are not smarter than Isaac Newton.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,720
So why did Newton lose
a fortune on one stock?

3
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:09,180
He wasn't alone. America's
most famous writer,

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,420
its greatest economist, and two
Nobel Prize winners all lost fortunes

5
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,880
too. Four geniuses.
The same four mistakes.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,300
And at the end, you'll meet a
janitor who avoided all four,

7
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,540
and died with $8 million.

8
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:26,640
Four true stories. Let's start
with the smartest man of them all.

9
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:31,420
London, 1720.

10
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,400
Isaac Newton is 77 years old.

11
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:38,220
He has explained gravity, invented
calculus, and for more than twenty

12
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:40,200
years he has run the Royal Mint.

13
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,380
He literally makes England's money.

14
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:45,640
That year, all of London is
talking about one stock:

15
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:47,020
the South Sea Company.

16
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,180
Its shares change hands in the coffee
houses of a narrow street called

17
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,480
Exchange Alley. From lords to
servants, everybody wants in.

18
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,480
The company has a monopoly on
British trade with South America.

19
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:02,220
The trade itself earns almost nothing.

20
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:05,740
But the company has just made a deal
to take over the national debt,

21
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:07,760
and its share price is flying.

22
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,920
In January, one share costs about
a hundred and thirty pounds.

23
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,880
By March, it's three hundred and thirty.

24
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:19,280
Newton owns shares. In April he sells
them, for a profit of about twenty

25
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,140
thousand pounds. A fortune.

26
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,460
And a smart move.

27
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:24,580
Except the price doesn't stop.

28
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:27,180
By the end of May, it's
five hundred and fifty.

29
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,840
In early June, almost nine hundred.

30
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,840
And this is the part that hurts.

31
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:33,900
Newton's friends are still in.

32
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,420
Every week they get richer,
and he just watches.

33
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:41,160
So in June, the man who invented
calculus does something very human.

34
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,480
He buys back in. Near the top.

35
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:45,280
With almost everything he owns.

36
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,740
In August, one share touches £1,000.

37
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,460
By the end of September,
it's a hundred and fifty.

38
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,120
Thousands of investors are ruined.

39
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,980
Parliament investigates, and sends
the finance minister to the Tower

40
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,200
of London. The same fortress where
Newton's Mint makes its coins.

41
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:06,660
When it's over, his early profit is
gone, and so is about a third of

42
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,280
the fortune he started with.

43
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,840
He later wrote that the South
Sea had left his pockets empty.

44
00:02:12,159 --> 00:02:13,720
Notice what went wrong.

45
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,160
It wasn't his math. Newton
got out at the right time.

46
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:21,220
What he couldn't stand was watching
other people get rich without him.

47
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,460
Mistake number one: following the crowd.

48
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,980
Hartford, Connecticut, 1880.

49
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:32,300
Mark Twain is the most
famous writer in America.

50
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,980
Tom Sawyer is a hit.

51
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,960
Huckleberry Finn is on the way.

52
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:37,440
The money is pouring in.

53
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:40,700
And Twain loves one thing
almost as much as writing:

54
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:41,800
new inventions.

55
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:46,060
By his own account, he was once
offered stock in a strange new gadget,

56
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,920
and said no. The gadget was the telephone.

57
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:52,080
Then he meets an inventor
named James Paige.

58
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,380
Paige is building a machine
that sets type for printing,

59
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:58,360
automatically, faster than any human can.

60
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:02,540
Twain started out as a printer's
apprentice, setting type by hand.

61
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:05,040
He knows exactly how big this could be.

62
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:07,460
Every newspaper on Earth would want one.

63
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:09,280
And he has money to spend.

64
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:13,640
His own publishing company releases
the memoirs of President Ulysses

65
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:16,260
Grant, and they become a giant bestseller.

66
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:18,600
So he invests. Then he invests again.

67
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:20,520
And again. For 14 years.

68
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,180
There's just one problem.

69
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,700
The machine has around 18,000 parts,

70
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,320
and it keeps breaking down.

71
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:29,140
Paige keeps taking it
apart to make it perfect.

72
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,240
Every year, it's almost ready.

73
00:03:31,477 --> 00:03:35,460
Meanwhile, a simpler machine called
the Linotype is already at work in

74
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,700
newspaper offices.

75
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:40,755
By 1894, Twain has put in
about three hundred thousand

76
00:03:40,775 --> 00:03:43,940
dollars, roughly ten
million in today's money.

77
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:45,740
The machine is a failure.

78
00:03:46,220 --> 00:03:48,580
That same year his publishing
company collapses,

79
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,000
and Mark Twain files for bankruptcy.

80
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,700
He is almost sixty. So he sets off
on a lecture tour around the entire

81
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,440
world, to earn the money back.

82
00:03:57,660 --> 00:03:59,840
The law said he didn't
have to repay his debts.

83
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,000
He paid every dollar anyway.

84
00:04:02,318 --> 00:04:06,260
Today, the only surviving machine
sits in his old house in Hartford.

85
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,420
As a museum piece.

86
00:04:08,556 --> 00:04:10,240
Twain didn't follow a crowd.

87
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:11,840
His mistake was different.

88
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,323
He fell in love with one
idea, and kept feeding it.

89
00:04:15,343 --> 00:04:18,140
Mistake number two: all
your eggs in one basket.

90
00:04:21,486 --> 00:04:24,480
Now, you could say Newton and
Twain weren't money experts.

91
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:25,600
This man was.

92
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:30,140
Irving Fisher is a professor at Yale,
and the most famous economist in

93
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:34,460
America. Other great economists would
later call him the best the country

94
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:35,480
ever produced.

95
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:40,040
He's also rich. He invented a
filing system for index cards,

96
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:42,220
sold it, and put the money into stocks.

97
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,900
And in the 1920s, stocks
only seem to go up.

98
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,600
Fisher's fortune reportedly
reaches about $10 million.

99
00:04:50,023 --> 00:04:52,540
But he isn't only investing his own money.

100
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:54,860
He's also investing borrowed money.

101
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:56,220
That's called leverage.

102
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,920
In the 1920s, you could buy
stocks with as little as ten

103
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:00,860
percent down.

104
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:02,340
Here's how it works.

105
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:05,360
Put in $10 of your own, and borrow ninety.

106
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,840
If the stock rises 10%,
you've doubled your money.

107
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:11,660
If it falls 10%, you've lost all of it.

108
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:15,920
On October fifteenth, 1929,
Fisher announces that stock

109
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:20,080
prices have reached, quote, what
looks like a permanently high plateau.

110
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,280
Nine days later, the market crashes.

111
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:27,180
A few days after that, on two days in
a row, the Dow loses nearly a quarter

112
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:28,260
of its value.

113
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:32,400
For months, Fisher keeps telling
investors that the recovery is just

114
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,620
around the corner. He holds on.

115
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,300
It isn't. By the summer of 1932,

116
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:41,080
the Dow Jones has fallen
89% from its peak.

117
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:43,080
Fisher's fortune is gone.

118
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:46,580
He ends up deep in debt,
borrowing from his sister-in-law.

119
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:50,040
In the end, Yale has to buy his
house and rent it back to him,

120
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:51,600
so he has somewhere to live.

121
00:05:51,965 --> 00:05:55,400
He spends the rest of his life
working out why the Depression became

122
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:59,240
so deep. His answer, a theory
called debt deflation,

123
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,720
is still used by economists today.

124
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:03,860
He just had to live through it first.

125
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:07,360
The best economist in the
country was wiped out.

126
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:09,600
Not because he bought terrible companies.

127
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:12,820
Because he bought them with borrowed
money, and was sure he couldn't

128
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:13,560
be wrong.

129
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:15,600
Mistake number three: leverage.

130
00:06:19,043 --> 00:06:22,840
Surely by the 1990s, with
computers and modern math,

131
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:24,800
the geniuses had it figured out.

132
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:29,520
In 1994, a star Wall Street bond
trader named John Meriwether

133
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:32,920
starts a fund called
Long-Term Capital Management.

134
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:36,840
His team may be the smartest
ever assembled in finance.

135
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:41,260
It includes Myron Scholes and
Robert Merton, who in 1997

136
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:44,040
share the Nobel Prize in economics.

137
00:06:44,447 --> 00:06:46,340
Their strategy sounds boring.

138
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:50,140
They find two almost identical bonds
with slightly different prices,

139
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,180
and bet that the gap will close.

140
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:54,000
Each bet earns pennies.

141
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:56,480
To turn pennies into
fortunes, they borrow.

142
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:57,220
A lot.

143
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:01,160
And it works. The fund
returns 21% in its first year.

144
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:03,420
Then forty-three. Then forty-one.

145
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:07,600
By the start of 1998, the
fund has under five billion

146
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:11,760
dollars of its own, and
about $125 billion borrowed.

147
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:16,160
That's more than $25 of debt
for every dollar it owns.

148
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:18,080
Remember Fisher's math.

149
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:22,200
At that level, a drop of just
4% in what the fund owns wipes

150
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:22,940
it out.

151
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:27,120
Their models, built on years of
market data, say a loss like that is

152
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:28,500
almost impossible.

153
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,060
Then, in August 1998, Russia
defaults on its debt.

154
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:35,080
Investors everywhere panic.

155
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:38,880
And the price gaps that were
supposed to close get wider instead.

156
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:43,560
In less than four months,
the fund loses $4.6 billion.

157
00:07:43,911 --> 00:07:46,640
And because it owes so
much, to so many banks,

158
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:49,260
its collapse could take
Wall Street down with it.

159
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,940
So the Federal Reserve Bank of New
York gathers the big banks in one

160
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:57,020
room. Fourteen of them put
in $3.6 billion to take

161
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:57,920
the fund over.

162
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:02,180
The partners had about $1.9
billion of their own money

163
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,420
in the fund. It's wiped out.

164
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,360
The fund is closed in early 2000.

165
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:09,560
Meriwether starts a second fund.

166
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:12,380
It gets hit hard in the 2008 crisis,

167
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:13,700
and closes too.

168
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:17,460
The best-known book about them
is called When Genius Failed.

169
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:21,640
They trusted their models so much,
they forgot a model can be wrong.

170
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,920
Mistake number four: overconfidence.

171
00:08:27,392 --> 00:08:32,360
So. A physicist, a writer, an
economist, and a room full of PhDs.

172
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,760
Three centuries. Look at what they share.

173
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:37,520
None of them lost money
because they were stupid.

174
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:39,760
They lost it because they were smart.

175
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:41,440
Smart enough to feel sure.

176
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:43,420
Newton followed the crowd.

177
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:45,800
Twain bet everything on one idea.

178
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:47,740
Fisher borrowed to buy more.

179
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:50,400
And Long-Term Capital believed
it couldn't be wrong.

180
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:52,340
The crowd. One basket.

181
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:54,320
Leverage. Overconfidence.

182
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:58,960
Almost every investing disaster in
history is some mix of those four.

183
00:08:59,268 --> 00:09:00,680
And they still work today.

184
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:04,240
The crowd is the stock everyone
at work is talking about.

185
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:07,300
One basket is all your
savings in a single company,

186
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:11,040
or a single coin. Leverage
is trading on margin.

187
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:14,300
And overconfidence is the
thought: this time, I know.

188
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:16,600
So what does the opposite look like?

189
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:19,260
Meet the least likely
millionaire in America.

190
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,120
Brattleboro, Vermont.

191
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,240
Ronald Read pumps gas for 25 years.

192
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:31,160
Then he works as a janitor in a
department store for seventeen more.

193
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:33,180
He drives a used car.

194
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:35,760
He holds his coat together
with a safety pin.

195
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:40,040
Once, a stranger pays for his
breakfast, thinking he can't afford it.

196
00:09:40,179 --> 00:09:43,020
When he dies in 2014, at 92,

197
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:46,880
his town finds out he was
worth almost $8 million.

198
00:09:47,314 --> 00:09:49,520
No formulas. No borrowed money.

199
00:09:49,540 --> 00:09:53,420
No hot tips. He bought shares
in boring, solid companies.

200
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:55,520
Soap. Medicine. Electricity.

201
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:57,220
At least 95 of them.

202
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:59,420
He reinvested every dividend.

203
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:03,200
And he held on. Some of his
shares, for more than 50 years.

204
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:06,700
He kept his stock certificates
in a safe deposit box.

205
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:10,340
When it was opened, the
pile was five inches thick.

206
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:12,000
Check him against the list.

207
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:13,760
The crowd? He ignored it.

208
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:15,900
One basket? He had 95.

209
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:17,780
Leverage? None. Overconfidence?

210
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:19,960
He never tried to outsmart anyone.

211
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:24,220
He left $6 million to his
town's hospital and library.

212
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,000
The largest gifts either
had ever received.

213
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:29,900
Newton could calculate
the path of a comet.

214
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:31,600
Ronald Read could wait.

215
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:33,360
In the market, waiting won.

216
00:10:33,645 --> 00:10:35,980
You don't have to be a
genius to build wealth.

217
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:37,280
You have to be patient.

218
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:39,540
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

219
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:42,060
The next true money story is on its way.
